WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:10.370
Around 600 BC lived
Thales of Miletus,

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:14.690
widely regarded as the
first Greek philosopher,

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:17.690
as he was the first to give
a purely natural explanation

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:18.920
of the phenomena he observed.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.800
A key observation
that he made was

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.270
that certain stones-- such as
amber-- when rubbed against fur

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.980
would exhibit a
strange property.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.900
The amber seemed to emit
an invisible force which

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.465
would attract small fibers.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.470
He assumed this rendered
the amber magnetic,

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.302
another force he observed when
playing with lodestones, which

00:00:46.302 --> 00:00:47.635
are naturally occurring magnets.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.320
Many after him observed that
the contact or friction with fur

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.310
seemed to create an imbalance.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.060
Something was
pulled from the fur

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.990
and transferred
onto other objects.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.489
Now, not only did this result in
a small attractive or repulsive

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:10.500
force, but also in the
potential for shocks to occur.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.860
Once the discharge occurred,
the force disappeared.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.430
So the shock was some
form of discharge

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.000
which reversed the imbalance
created by the friction.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.010
Throughout history, we
were also fascinated

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:28.850
with lightning bolts, nature's
most passionate displays

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:31.020
of power and aggression.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.850
Most cultures assumed
this was a divine force,

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:35.684
outside the reach
of human hands,

00:01:35.684 --> 00:01:37.350
and was therefore
reserved for the gods.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.860
Up until the 17th century,
our descriptions of it

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.561
varied from an invisible,
intangible, inponderable agent,

00:01:47.561 --> 00:01:52.940
or even threads of syrup
which elongate and contract.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:56.550
And it was Benjamin Franklin
who, in 1750, set out

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.570
to prove that there was a
connection between lightning

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:04.040
and these tiny shocks
due to friction.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.310
In the famously dangerous
experiment done alone

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:11.250
with his son, he led a
kite into a thunderstorm.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.820
And near the bottom,
where the thread was wet,

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.440
he tied an iron key.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.100
And after some time, he brought
his knuckle up to be key,

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.800
and experienced a
series small shocks,

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.770
identical to the ones
created by contact with fur.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.700
This showed that,
indeed, lightning

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.710
was simply the same thing
as these household shocks,

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.800
but on a massive scale.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.970
And at this time, people had
begun to divide materials

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.640
into two categories.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.900
One were objects which
would allow, or accept,

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.600
discharge-- such
as gold or copper--

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.830
which we call
electrical conductors.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.450
And interestingly,
these materials

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:52.280
are also generally good
at conducting heat.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.630
And number two were
objects which would not

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.360
allow this discharge--
such as rubber,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.170
or electrical insulators.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:04.850
These materials also seemed to
insulate the transfer of heat.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:08.090
And we also began trying to
measure this force that Thales

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.250
had encountered.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.990
One way to do this
was to suspend

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:16.580
a piece of spongy plant, called
a pith ball, from a thread.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.880
And when we rubbed an
insulator against fur

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.230
and brought it
near the pith ball,

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.230
it would pull on it,
causing a deflection.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.350
If we added more
objects, we noticed

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:36.625
this deflection increased, due
to a greater pulling force.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:46.920
We also notice that the shape
of insulators made a difference.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.190
Large, thin insulators seemed to
exhibit a much stronger force.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.600
And amazingly, it was found
that conductors, such as copper

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.900
wire, would transmit this
pulling effect over a distance.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.480
This was demonstrated
by running a long wire

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.560
between the pith ball and
the charged insulator.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:18.120
When the object was
brought near the wire,

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.100
it pulled through the wire
and deflected the pith ball

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:23.576
instantly.

00:04:23.576 --> 00:04:26.430
When we later touch the
wire with our finger,

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.820
a discharge occurs
and the pulling stops,

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:31.630
and the ball is released.

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:33.590
Immediately, people
began speculating

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:37.420
that this could be the
future of optical telegraphs.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:42.070
And in 1774, French inventor
Georges-Louis Le Sage

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:45.630
was one of the first on record
to actually set up this idea.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:48.970
He sent messages through
an array of 26 wires,

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:52.450
each wire representing a
letter of the alphabet.

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:54.380
When a discharge
occurred at one end,

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.840
the pith ball would
move at the other.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:59.040
The trouble with his
telegraph was that it only

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.520
extended between the
two rooms of his house.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.570
The power of the deflection
was small and difficult to work

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:05.347
with.

00:05:05.347 --> 00:05:07.680
Though at the time, people
were investigating techniques

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.810
for generating larger
charge differences in order

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.110
to amplify the force involved.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:16.210
One improvement popularized
by Alessandro Volta

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:19.780
one year later was an
easy way of generating

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.850
discharges on demand.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:24.900
It was based on the idea that
a charged insulator could

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:29.630
introduce, or transfer, a charge
onto a nearby conducting plate.

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:31.490
One needed to merely
bring the metal plate

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.750
close to the insulator, which
would pull on the charge

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:37.140
distribution in the
metal plate, resulting

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.690
in an imbalance, or electrical
tension, in the metal plate.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:42.770
Then one could bring
their finger to the plate,

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.750
and a discharge would occur.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:48.340
Then the plate is pulled away
using an insulating handle,

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:52.560
and an excess charge would
remain trapped in the plate.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.460
The plate could then
be discharged at will

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:57.490
simply by touching it to a
conductor, such as a finger.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:00.780
And amazingly, this process
can be repeated many times

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.210
without recharging
the insulating plate.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.870
We could then generate many
small discharges at will.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:08.640
And by now, Benjamin
Franklin was

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.700
focused on finding
out how to trap

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.250
or store up these discharges.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:15.610
At this time, he still
assumed that electricity

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.820
was some sort of
invisible fluid,

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.140
since he knew it could
travel through water.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:23.860
So he assumed that water
inside an insulator

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:26.070
could hold electricity.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.120
What we now call the
Leyden jar was a glass

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.580
jar with water
inside and a metal

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:32.880
probe running out the top.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.865
Franklin also wrapped the
outside in a conductive metal.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.830
When he brought a
charged conductor

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.230
towards the top
probe, a discharge

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.460
would occur, and stay
trapped in the jar.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.250
More importantly
was that the jar

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:51.060
could be charged multiple times.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.112
Each spark would amplify
the charge separation,

00:06:54.112 --> 00:06:57.280
or electrical tension,
inside the jar.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.060
A good analogy is to think
of the jar as a balloon,

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:03.530
and each discharge as
a short jolt of water.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:07.165
After hundreds of iterations,
the tension becomes massive.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:15.370
And to release the
charge, he simply

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:18.570
touched the outside
conductor to the top probe.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:24.580
A large discharge occurred.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:27.160
Franklin improved
the design over time,

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.450
eventually realizing
that the charge was not

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:32.530
stored in the water,
but the glass.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:34.780
The water was merely
a conductive path

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:36.767
from the probe to the jar.

00:07:36.767 --> 00:07:38.225
Today, we would
call the Leyden jar

00:07:38.225 --> 00:07:41.710
a capacitor, or a
charge-storing device.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:43.760
And when he chained
many jars together,

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.270
he founded he could increase
the capacity even more,

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.650
and release deadly
bolts of electricity.

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:51.910
And over the years, people
focused on more effective ways

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:55.320
of building up charge using
friction machines which

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.900
could be stored in
capacitors and released

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:01.120
as spectacular displays
of man-made lightning.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.240
And over the next
50 years, people

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:28.500
tried to design systems
for sending sparks

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:32.730
across greater distances using
longer wires and more powerful

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:33.230
discharges.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:38.590
However, sending
electrostatic discharges

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:41.809
as a communication
method seemed clumsy,

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:44.750
archaic, and was no
improvement over the existing

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:48.670
optical telegraphs of the day.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:52.850
They were widely ignored
by government and industry.

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:56.990
Though tides were rising--
an electric revolution

00:08:56.990 --> 00:08:59.670
was just around the corner.

